1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,598
The first great idea in CS61C is abstraction.

2
00:00:09,598 --> 00:00:15,100
A computer system is built in layers, from the top all the way down.

3
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,061
At the top is a high-level language, such as C.

4
00:00:18,061 --> 00:00:24,363
A compiler translates it into assembly language, and an assembler then turns that into machine code,

5
00:00:24,363 --> 00:00:28,400
which is the zeros and ones the machine can read directly.

6
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:29,862
Below that is hardware.

7
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:35,141
It starts with an architecture described by a block diagram, in which, for example,

8
00:00:35,141 --> 00:00:38,966
the values of two registers flow into an adder.

9
00:00:38,966 --> 00:00:42,282
Zoom in on the adder, and inside are logic gates.

10
00:00:42,282 --> 00:00:47,424
To add two bits, an XOR gate gives the sum, and an AND gate gives the carry.

11
00:00:47,424 --> 00:00:53,733
Zoom in on one gate, and it is built from transistors, which is the lowest layer of all.

12
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:57,978
Between every two neighboring layers there is a clearly defined interface.

13
00:00:57,978 --> 00:01:03,966
As long as you respect the interface, you don't need to care about the details of the layer below.

14
00:01:03,966 --> 00:01:11,456
This series is about the interface between software and hardware, which is known as the instruction set architecture.

15
00:01:11,456 --> 00:01:13,666
We call it the ISA for short.

16
00:01:13,666 --> 00:01:19,938
The ISA specifies which instructions assembly language has, and how they are encoded as bits,

17
00:01:19,938 --> 00:01:28,200
as well as architectural features such as registers, data types, memory addressing, and input and output.

18
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:36,200
Programs and compilers are written to the ISA, and hardware such as the CPU is designed to the same ISA.

19
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,638
So a program can run on any CPU that follows the same ISA.

20
00:01:40,638 --> 00:01:46,000
The ISA we will study in this series is called RISC-V.

21
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,016
Most assembly is generated by compilers, and hardly anyone writes it by hand.

22
00:01:51,016 --> 00:01:52,433
So why learn it?

23
00:01:52,433 --> 00:01:59,683
A post on Slashdot in two thousand four even claimed that whether a programmer is mediocre or excellent depends on

24
00:01:59,683 --> 00:02:01,782
whether they understand assembly.

25
00:02:01,782 --> 00:02:09,287
Understanding assembly means understanding how a computer executes instructions, and it lets you write faster, leaner,

26
00:02:09,287 --> 00:02:13,766
and cheaper programs, even in a high-level language.

27
00:02:13,766 --> 00:02:20,309
Before studying an ISA, let's look at the basic layout of a computer, the von Neumann architecture.

28
00:02:20,309 --> 00:02:27,233
The processor, or CPU, does the computing, and it has two parts, the control unit and the datapath.

29
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:32,630
The stars of the datapath are the registers and the arithmetic logic unit, or ALU.

30
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:37,171
Outside the processor are the memory, which stores programs and data,

31
00:02:37,171 --> 00:02:41,515
and input and output devices such as the keyboard and the display.

32
00:02:41,515 --> 00:02:47,333
Control, datapath, memory, input, and output are the five components of a computer.

33
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:50,931
The processor sends out an address to read or write memory,

34
00:02:50,931 --> 00:02:56,666
and an enable signal makes sure that a read can't change memory by mistake.

35
00:02:56,666 --> 00:02:58,433
The processor is very fast.

36
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:03,928
In a four gigahertz processor, one clock cycle lasts only a quarter of a nanosecond.

37
00:03:03,928 --> 00:03:05,105
How short is that?

38
00:03:05,105 --> 00:03:09,358
Light travels about three hundred thousand kilometers per second,

39
00:03:09,358 --> 00:03:14,766
and in a quarter of a nanosecond it covers only seven and a half centimeters.

40
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:21,204
Even if the data is only ten centimeters away, light needs about three tenths of a nanosecond to get there,

41
00:03:21,204 --> 00:03:22,949
which is longer than a cycle.

42
00:03:22,949 --> 00:03:27,200
So the data has to be very close to the processor.

43
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:31,741
So a modern computer has at least two kinds of hardware for storing data.

44
00:03:31,741 --> 00:03:37,341
The first is registers, which sit inside the processor, are small, and are lightning fast.

45
00:03:37,341 --> 00:03:41,945
The second is memory, which sits outside the processor and is much larger,

46
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:49,100
but each access takes about a hundred nanoseconds, or four hundred cycles.

47
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:50,445
Now compare capacity.

48
00:03:50,445 --> 00:03:55,825
Thirty-two registers of four bytes each add up to only a hundred twenty-eight bytes.

49
00:03:55,825 --> 00:04:03,300
A laptop, on the other hand, has two to sixty-four gigabytes of memory, and a server can have up to a terabyte.

50
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:06,963
Let's draw those hundred twenty-eight bytes as a small square.

51
00:04:06,963 --> 00:04:12,635
At the same scale, sixty-four gigabytes of memory has about five hundred million times the area,

52
00:04:12,635 --> 00:04:16,766
and the small square would be less than one pixel.

53
00:04:16,766 --> 00:04:21,948
Main memory is usually DRAM on a separate chip, such as DDR memory or HBM,

54
00:04:21,948 --> 00:04:25,380
and a few tens of dollars buys several gigabytes.

55
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,161
But the laws of physics say that smaller means faster,

56
00:04:29,161 --> 00:04:35,300
and a register is roughly fifty to five hundred times faster than DRAM.

57
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,604
Let's borrow an analogy from Jim Gray.

58
00:04:37,604 --> 00:04:43,485
Getting data from a register is like recalling something in your head, and that takes one minute.

59
00:04:43,485 --> 00:04:49,666
Memory that is a hundred times slower is like driving to Sacramento to fetch a paper you forgot.

60
00:04:49,666 --> 00:04:55,514
And if it is five hundred times slower, you would have to drive all the way to Los Angeles and back,

61
00:04:55,514 --> 00:04:59,666
just to fetch a single piece of data!

62
00:04:59,666 --> 00:05:06,409
Registers are few, and they share precious chip area with the processor core, so they are very expensive.

63
00:05:06,409 --> 00:05:09,812
That is why designing an ISA is like dancing a tango,

64
00:05:09,812 --> 00:05:17,466
where we compute in registers as much as possible and make as few trips to memory and disk as we can.

65
00:05:17,466 --> 00:05:19,283
How should an ISA be designed?

66
00:05:19,283 --> 00:05:24,553
In the seventies and eighties the trend was to make instructions more and more complex,

67
00:05:24,553 --> 00:05:28,913
so that a single instruction could read memory, compute, and write back.

68
00:05:28,913 --> 00:05:35,515
Programs got shorter and could make fewer memory accesses, but the price was complicated, expensive hardware.

69
00:05:35,515 --> 00:05:40,800
These architectures were later called CISC, or complex instruction set computers.

70
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:49,108
In the early eighties, John Cocke at IBM designed the IBM eight oh one, the first reduced instruction set computer,

71
00:05:49,108 --> 00:05:50,066
or RISC.

72
00:05:50,066 --> 00:05:51,726
The idea was the opposite.

73
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:58,240
The instruction set is small and simple, and complex operations are left to software and the compiler,

74
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:01,050
which build them out of simple instructions.

75
00:06:01,050 --> 00:06:09,900
There are more instructions, but simple hardware can execute many more of them every second, so overall it is faster.

76
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:15,356
Dave Patterson at Berkeley and John Hennessy at Stanford pushed the idea to its limit,

77
00:06:15,356 --> 00:06:18,782
and built the RISC and MIPS projects at the same time.

78
00:06:18,782 --> 00:06:23,731
All three pioneers later won the Turing Award, Cocke in nineteen eighty-seven,

79
00:06:23,731 --> 00:06:27,066
and Patterson and Hennessy in twenty seventeen.

80
00:06:27,066 --> 00:06:31,462
Today both RISC and CISC are popular and are developing fast.

81
00:06:31,462 --> 00:06:38,466
Many Windows computers use x86, and many phones and Apple's own chips use ARM.

82
00:06:38,466 --> 00:06:46,717
Teaching needs an ISA, and x86 is too complicated, while old or homemade ISAs lack real compilers and software.

83
00:06:46,717 --> 00:06:54,744
RISC-V was born in two thousand ten, in the Par Lab at UC Berkeley, started by Patterson and Krste Asanovic.

84
00:06:54,744 --> 00:07:00,733
By around two thousand twenty, even MIPS had moved over to it.

85
00:07:00,733 --> 00:07:02,860
It is popular for two big reasons.

86
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:09,930
It is open source, and it carries no license fees, so anyone can use it for free, whether for teaching, research,

87
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:10,680
or business.

88
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:14,747
A worldwide community of academia and industry drives it forward,

89
00:07:14,747 --> 00:07:23,233
and people have already used it to build everything from embedded microcontrollers to warehouse-scale supercomputers.

90
00:07:23,233 --> 00:07:29,044
RISC-V comes in thirty-two, sixty-four, and one hundred twenty-eight bit variants.

91
00:07:29,044 --> 00:07:34,289
This series covers RV32I, the thirty-two bit base integer instruction set,

92
00:07:34,289 --> 00:07:38,900
and things like multiplication live in extensions such as M.

93
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:44,277
The whole architecture definition fits on a single page, called the green card,

94
00:07:44,277 --> 00:07:49,313
named after the famous IBM three sixty green card of the nineteen sixties.

95
00:07:49,313 --> 00:07:55,711
It is simple and elegant, and good for learning assembly and for learning to design computers,

96
00:07:55,711 --> 00:07:58,298
which is exactly why CS61C teaches it.

97
00:07:58,298 --> 00:08:00,066
Go Bears!

98
00:08:00,066 --> 00:08:02,626
The series has fourteen episodes in all.

99
00:08:02,626 --> 00:08:10,433
In the next one, we start from a single line of C and see how it becomes assembly, and we meet the RISC-V registers.

